`SubscriptionPlan` is a `TypedDict`. Instances are plain `dict` objects at runtime (both `SubscriptionPlan(...)` and `TypeAdapter.validate_python` return a `dict`), so this method never runs. I checked: `str(SubscriptionPlan(plan='a', ...))` prints `{'plan': 'a', ...}`. The method is misleading dead code, and type checkers will also flag methods inside a TypedDict body.

::diff{file="api/services/billing_service.py" lines="28-30"}

Remove it. If you need a debug representation, add a module-level helper function.

The code builds `plans_to_cache` and uses it to guard the write, but then loops over `bulk_plans` and logs `len(bulk_plans)`. Today the two dicts hold the same entries, so `plans_to_cache` does nothing. If someone later adds filtering to `plans_to_cache` (for example, dropping `None` plans), the write loop will silently ignore it. The method also calls `BillingService.get_plan_bulk` instead of `cls.get_plan_bulk`, which doesn't match the rest of the classmethods.

::diff{file="api/services/billing_service.py" lines="358-385"}

Iterate `plans_to_cache` in the pipeline loop and log message, and only add a plan to it when the plan is truthy.
